assistantMessageEvent.content:
This is a recent, big commit. The current code matches the upstream style. Now, the bug report is about compose-refs' setRef / React 19's infinite update loop. The current compose-refs code already handles the cleanup...

Wait, let me reread the current compose-refs code very carefully:

```tsx
function setRef<T>(ref: PossibleRef<T>, value: T) {
  if (typeof ref === 'function') {
    return ref(value);
  } else if (ref !== null && ref !== undefined) {
    ref.current = value;
  }
}

function composeRefs<T>(...refs: PossibleRef<T>[]): React.RefCallback<T> {
  return (node) => {
    let hasCleanup = false;
    const cleanups = refs.map((ref) => {
      const cleanup = setRef(ref, node);
      if (!hasCleanup && typeof cleanup == 'function') {
        hasCleanup = true;
      }
      return cleanup;
    });

    if (hasCleanup) {
      return () => {
        for (let i = 0; i < cleanups.length; i++) {
          const cleanup = cleanups[i];
          if (typeof cleanup == 'function') {
            cleanup();
          } else {
            setRef(refs[i], null);
          }
        }
      };
    }
  };
}
```

This looks correct. Hmm.

But the reported stack trace:
```
at setRef (composeRefs.tsx:11:5)
at composeRefs.tsx:22:45
at Array.forEach (<anonymous>)
```

Line 22 is `const cleanups = refs.map(...)` — the stack trace says `forEach`, so this is compose-refs 1.1.0 (the version the reporter is using), not this repo's 1.1.2. In this repo, 1.1.2 already contains the fix. So the compose-refs package here is already fixed.

Therefore, the remaining bug should be somewhere else — in slot, or in some other package. The test command is `pnpm vitest run packages/react/slot`. That means the regression test goes in the slot package. So there's probably a real bug in the React 19 slot that triggers an infinite update loop.

Let me think about React 19-specific infinite loop scenarios in slot.tsx:

